WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.450
Change one source string, and nothing tells you
the French, German and Japanese versions are now wrong.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.100
So they ship anyway.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:14.600
LingoTracker keeps a status next to every translation,
right in your Git repo.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:19.850
One package gives you a CLI, a REST API,
and a local web UI.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.900
No hosted service. No account.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.400
Change a source value, and its translations turn stale.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.400
Untranslated ones stay new.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.050
Then one command gates the release.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.400
Validate fails your build on anything
missing, stale, or unreviewed.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.600
LingoTracker supports ICU messages,
and checks them under each locale,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.900
so a broken plural is caught before it renders blank.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.550
The web UI runs on that same API.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.300
Browse the tree, filter by status, and search everything.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.400
Translators get exactly what needs work, as XLIFF or JSON.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.100
Import their files back,
and renamed placeholders are repaired.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.950
Bundle writes JSON for each locale, and can emit typed keys.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.200
So a mistyped key fails to compile.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.900
Install the skill, and your AI assistant
finds hardcoded strings,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.900
reuses keys, and updates your components.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.200
One thousand commits later.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.400
This is LingoTracker 1.0.
